# __NAME__

A mySMB marketplace plugin scaffolded by [`create-mysmb-plugin`](https://github.com/mySMB-AI-Studio/mysmb-marketplace).

## Quick start

```
npm install
npm run dev
```

Then open [http://localhost:5173](http://localhost:5173) — that's the **widget harness**. Pick a template from the sidebar, edit the JSON, watch it render live. Switch to the **Connections** tab to manage MCP servers — OAuth login, paste-token fallback, and a full tool inspector are all in there.

## The harness, in 30 seconds

- **Editor tab** — three panes: templates list (your widgets + every marketplace plugin's widgets), JSON source, live preview at any dashboard size. The strip at the bottom mirrors connection state without leaving the tab.
- **Connections tab** — every MCP server from your `plugin/.mcp.json` plus every marketplace plugin's `.mcp.json` shows up here. Click **Login with OAuth** to run the spec-compliant MCP 2.1 flow (PKCE + dynamic client registration); credentials persist to `.harness-cache/oauth/<server>.json` so you only authorize once. There's a "Paste token" fallback for servers that don't advertise discovery.
- **System primitives** — the harness imports MyHub's `widgets-system/system` live, so widgets render with the exact components production uses (Card, Stack, Icon, Skeleton, etc.). Point `HARNESS_MYHUB_PATH` at your `myHubV2` checkout for full fidelity, or let the harness fall back to a local stub (a plain Tailwind look that's enough to verify layout).

## Build it with Claude

In another terminal, from this folder:

```
claude
```

That starts Claude Code in this project. The `.claude/` folder ships with the skills Claude needs to author widgets, skills, agents, and `$computed` helpers for you. Try:

> "Add a widget that shows my open invoices."
> "Add a skill that creates an invoice."
> "I need a `$computed` that formats Australian phone numbers."

Claude will read the relevant skill, write the file, and tell you to reload the harness.

## What's in here

- **`plugin/`** — the plugin you're building. This is the only folder that ends up in the marketplace.
- **`harness/`** — local-only dev sandbox: a Vite + React widget renderer with an MCP client. Reads `plugin/widgets/*.json` and `plugin/.mcp.json`.
- **`.claude/`** — skills and agents that teach Claude Code how to build mySMB plugins.
- **`CLAUDE.md`** — project orientation that Claude reads on first turn.

## Setting credentials

Two paths, depending on your MCP server.

**OAuth (preferred)** — for any HTTP/SSE server that advertises [RFC 8414](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8414) discovery (`/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server/<path>`) and supports [RFC 7591](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7591) dynamic client registration. Click **Login with OAuth** on the server card in the Connections tab; the harness handles PKCE, registration, callback, and token persistence. Tokens land in `.harness-cache/oauth/<server>.json` (gitignored).

**Environment vars** — for stdio servers or HTTP servers that take a static API key. `${VAR}` placeholders in `plugin/.mcp.json` are substituted from your shell:

**macOS / Linux**
```
export __ENV_PREFIX___API_TOKEN=xxx
npm run dev
```

**Windows PowerShell**
```
$env:__ENV_PREFIX___API_TOKEN = "xxx"
npm run dev
```

Every `${VAR}` you reference in `.mcp.json` must also appear in `plugin/README.md` under a `## Configuration` heading, so users (and the validator) know what to set.

**Paste token** — last resort. The Connections tab has a "Paste token" button on every HTTP/SSE server card; the token is saved to `.harness-cache/tokens.json` (gitignored) and sent as `Authorization: Bearer <token>` on every request.

## Pointing at MyHub for full fidelity

The harness reuses MyHub's `widgets-system/system` directly. Discovery order:

1. `HARNESS_MYHUB_PATH` env var (absolute path to your `myHubV2` checkout)
2. Walk up the directory tree looking for `apps/web/src/features/widgets-system`
3. Sibling layout: `../../../../../myHubV2` from the harness `src/`
4. Fall back to the local `system-fallback/` (plain Tailwind look, every component name supported)

The fallback is enough for layout verification. If a widget references a `$computed` helper or component that isn't in the fallback, it renders as a yellow `?TypeName` chip so you can spot it.

## Publishing

When the plugin works the way you want:

1. Copy `plugin/` into the marketplace repo at `mySMB-Plugin-Marketplace/Plugins/plugins/__SLUG__/`.
2. Add an entry to `.claude-plugin/marketplace.json`.
3. From the marketplace root: `npx tsx scripts/validate.ts`.
4. Open a PR.

See [CREATING_PLUGINS.md](https://github.com/mySMB-AI-Studio/mysmb-marketplace/blob/main/CREATING_PLUGINS.md) in the marketplace repo for the full reference.
